Lab 2: Real-time Surveillance with Motion Detection
Objective
Capture live video from your webcam and implement motion detection using background subtraction in OpenCV — all on your local Ubuntu machine without cloud connectivity.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure Python and OpenCV are installed:

pip install opencv-python
sudo apt install python3-opencv -y
Make sure your machine has a webcam connected and recognized. You can test it using:

cheese
Steps
Step 1: Capture Video Feed from Webcam
Create a working directory and script:

mkdir -p ~/opencv-surveillance-lab
cd ~/opencv-surveillance-lab
nano motion_detection.py
Add this basic webcam capture code:

import cv2

cap = cv2.VideoCapture(0)  # 0 = default webcam

if not cap.isOpened():
    print("Cannot open webcam")
    exit()

while True:
    ret, frame = cap.read()
    if not ret:
        break

    cv2.imshow('Webcam Feed', frame)

    # Exit on pressing 'q'
    if cv2.waitKey(1) & 0xFF == ord('q'):
        break

cap.release()
cv2.destroyAllWindows()
Run the script:

python3 motion_detection.py
Press q to exit the video stream.

Step 2: Add Motion Detection with Background Subtraction
Update motion_detection.py with background subtraction logic:

import cv2

cap = cv2.VideoCapture(0)
if not cap.isOpened():
    print("Cannot open webcam")
    exit()

# Create background subtractor
fgbg = cv2.createBackgroundSubtractorMOG2()

while True:
    ret, frame = cap.read()
    if not ret:
        break

    # Apply background subtraction
    fgmask = fgbg.apply(frame)

    # Show original frame and mask
    cv2.imshow('Live Feed', frame)
    cv2.imshow('Motion Mask', fgmask)

    if cv2.waitKey(1) & 0xFF == ord('q'):
        break

cap.release()
cv2.destroyAllWindows()
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Captured live video from your webcam using OpenCV

Applied background subtraction to detect motion in real-time

Displayed both the raw video and motion mask for surveillance simulation

This practical exercise gives you hands-on experience in building a foundational surveillance tool — all offline on your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu system.
